1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,243
Welcome to lectures thirteen and fourteen:

2
00:00:03,243 --> 00:00:04,711
data visualization.

3
00:00:04,711 --> 00:00:21,237
We will learn how charts are built using the Grammar of Graphics, how to tell good charts from misleading ones, how to visualize non-numeric data, how dashboards are organised, and how to turn a chart into a story.

4
00:00:21,237 --> 00:00:24,480
This also prepares you for assignment one.

5
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,013
Five ideas today.

6
00:00:27,013 --> 00:00:27,421
One:

7
00:00:27,421 --> 00:00:33,130
the Grammar of Graphics, building any chart from layers.

8
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:33,538
Two:

9
00:00:33,538 --> 00:00:36,597
good versus misleading charts.

10
00:00:36,597 --> 00:00:37,208
Three:

11
00:00:37,208 --> 00:00:45,365
visualizing non-numeric data such as categories, hierarchies, text and networks.

12
00:00:45,365 --> 00:00:45,874
Four:

13
00:00:45,874 --> 00:00:47,506
dashboard logic.

14
00:00:47,506 --> 00:00:48,423
And five:

15
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:50,768
storytelling with data.

16
00:00:51,568 --> 00:00:53,267
Why visualize at all?

17
00:00:53,267 --> 00:01:05,806
These four datasets, known as Anscombe's quartet, have the same mean, the same variance, the same correlation of point eight two, and the same fitted line.

18
00:01:05,806 --> 00:01:08,637
Yet they look completely different:

19
00:01:08,637 --> 00:01:16,727
a clean linear trend, a curve, a line with one outlier, and a vertical stack with one extreme point.

20
00:01:16,727 --> 00:01:21,662
Summary statistics compress, and compression hides structure.

21
00:01:21,662 --> 00:01:24,736
Only the picture shows the difference.

22
00:01:25,536 --> 00:01:31,028
The Grammar of Graphics says every chart is built from layers.

23
00:01:31,028 --> 00:01:33,774
The data is the table you plot.

24
00:01:33,774 --> 00:01:39,798
Aesthetics decide which column controls x, y, colour, size or shape.

25
00:01:39,798 --> 00:01:43,519
Geometry is the shape that draws the data:

26
00:01:43,519 --> 00:01:46,088
points, lines, bars or boxes.

27
00:01:46,088 --> 00:01:52,820
And scales, labels and theme control axis ranges, titles, colours and fonts.

28
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:58,224
If you can name the layers, you can build or debug any chart.

29
00:01:59,024 --> 00:02:02,776
Not all visual channels are equally accurate.

30
00:02:02,776 --> 00:02:13,533
People judge position on a common scale best, then length, then angle and slope, then area, and colour intensity least precisely.

31
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:19,703
That is why bar charts and dot plots beat pie charts for comparing values.

32
00:02:19,703 --> 00:02:25,040
Put your most important comparison on the most accurate channel.

33
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:30,850
Choose the geometry from the question you are asking.

34
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:33,686
How is one number distributed:

35
00:02:33,686 --> 00:02:36,616
histogram, density or box plot.

36
00:02:36,616 --> 00:02:39,074
How do two numbers relate:

37
00:02:39,074 --> 00:02:40,303
scatter plot.

38
00:02:40,303 --> 00:02:43,612
How does a number change over time:

39
00:02:43,612 --> 00:02:44,652
line chart.

40
00:02:44,652 --> 00:02:46,731
How do groups compare:

41
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:49,000
bar, box or violin plot.

42
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,836
How do two categories overlap:

43
00:02:51,836 --> 00:02:54,199
a heatmap of a cross-tab.

44
00:02:54,199 --> 00:02:56,090
And part of a whole:

45
00:02:56,090 --> 00:02:58,264
stacked bar or treemap.

46
00:02:59,064 --> 00:03:05,611
Facets, also called small multiples, repeat the same chart once per group in a grid.

47
00:03:05,611 --> 00:03:11,223
Every panel shares the same axes, so groups can be compared at a glance.

48
00:03:11,223 --> 00:03:24,552
It takes one line of code, for example col equals the grouping column in seaborn, and it is almost always better than piling many lines or colours onto one cluttered plot.

49
00:03:25,352 --> 00:03:28,384
Now, good versus misleading charts.

50
00:03:28,384 --> 00:03:32,195
The most common trick is the truncated axis.

51
00:03:32,195 --> 00:03:37,045
These two bar charts show exactly the same four numbers.

52
00:03:37,045 --> 00:03:45,273
On the left the axis starts at ninety five, so a five percent rise looks like a five-fold jump.

53
00:03:45,273 --> 00:03:51,423
On the right the axis starts at zero and shows the true, modest change.

54
00:03:51,423 --> 00:03:56,360
When bars encode a quantity, the axis must start at zero.

55
00:03:57,160 --> 00:04:00,373
Charts can also mislead in other ways.

56
00:04:00,373 --> 00:04:04,431
Dual axes can be scaled to fake any correlation.

57
00:04:04,431 --> 00:04:09,758
A cherry-picked range shows only the dates that suit the story.

58
00:04:09,758 --> 00:04:12,041
Distorted areas exaggerate:

59
00:04:12,041 --> 00:04:16,015
doubling a circle's radius quadruples its area.

60
00:04:16,015 --> 00:04:21,257
Clutter and three-D effects hide the data and bend the values.

61
00:04:21,257 --> 00:04:28,528
And missing context, with no baseline, sample size or source, leaves viewers guessing.

62
00:04:29,328 --> 00:04:31,872
Principles of an honest chart.

63
00:04:31,872 --> 00:04:36,876
Start bar charts at zero; line charts may zoom, but say so.

64
00:04:36,876 --> 00:04:41,286
Label axes with units and give an informative title.

65
00:04:41,286 --> 00:04:47,053
Use colour with purpose and check it works for colour-blind readers.

66
00:04:47,053 --> 00:04:54,262
Remove decoration that carries no data, such as heavy gridlines, three-D and shadows.

67
00:04:54,262 --> 00:04:58,248
And always show the sample size and the source.

68
00:04:59,048 --> 00:05:07,538
Mean and correlation do not apply to categories, hierarchies or text, so each needs its own picture.

69
00:05:07,538 --> 00:05:14,754
For categorical data, use a sorted bar chart, sorted by value and not alphabetically.

70
00:05:14,754 --> 00:05:22,310
For hierarchical data, use a treemap, where area shows share and nesting shows structure.

71
00:05:22,310 --> 00:05:27,913
For text, use a word cloud or, better, a word-frequency bar chart.

72
00:05:27,913 --> 00:05:35,384
And for relationships, use a network diagram, where nodes are items and edges are links.

73
00:05:36,184 --> 00:05:37,559
Dashboard logic.

74
00:05:37,559 --> 00:05:44,775
A dashboard is several panels sharing one figure, organised around a single purpose.

75
00:05:44,775 --> 00:05:49,844
Start with the audience and the decision they have to make.

76
00:05:49,844 --> 00:05:54,225
Put the key numbers first, in a row of K P I cards.

77
00:05:54,225 --> 00:05:56,631
Add supporting detail below:

78
00:05:56,631 --> 00:06:00,067
a trend, a breakdown and one comparison.

79
00:06:00,067 --> 00:06:07,627
And keep the design consistent, with the same colours, scales and filters across panels.

80
00:06:07,627 --> 00:06:10,720
Fewer panels, each with a clear job.

81
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:15,685
Storytelling with data turns a chart into a message.

82
00:06:15,685 --> 00:06:24,495
Let the title state the insight, such as Central's discount stands out, rather than just naming the variables.

83
00:06:24,495 --> 00:06:26,097
Highlight one thing:

84
00:06:26,097 --> 00:06:30,102
gray out the rest and colour the bar that matters.

85
00:06:30,102 --> 00:06:33,786
Annotate the key moment directly on the chart.

86
00:06:33,786 --> 00:06:34,907
And declutter:

87
00:06:34,907 --> 00:06:39,072
remove everything that does not support the message.

88
00:06:39,872 --> 00:06:43,230
A simple story structure has three parts.

89
00:06:43,230 --> 00:06:43,885
Context:

90
00:06:43,885 --> 00:06:47,570
what is the situation and why does it matter.

91
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:48,307
Conflict:

92
00:06:48,307 --> 00:06:52,647
what did the data reveal that is surprising or wrong.

93
00:06:52,647 --> 00:06:53,876
And resolution:

94
00:06:53,876 --> 00:06:58,298
what action do you recommend, and what will it change.

95
00:06:58,298 --> 00:07:04,112
Every chart in your presentation should serve one of these three parts.

96
00:07:04,912 --> 00:07:10,860
To prepare for assignment one, structure your presentation like this.

97
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:16,893
A problem statement with the question, unit of observation and target.

98
00:07:16,893 --> 00:07:18,014
A data audit:

99
00:07:18,014 --> 00:07:23,013
shape, types, missing values, duplicates and hidden zeros.

100
00:07:23,013 --> 00:07:27,323
Univariate and bivariate E D A with honest charts.

101
00:07:27,323 --> 00:07:31,288
Two or three hypotheses, labelled exploratory.

102
00:07:31,288 --> 00:07:39,046
And a story, with insight titles, one highlighted finding per slide, and a recommendation.

103
00:07:39,046 --> 00:07:45,424
Every chart needs an honest axis, labels with units, and an insight title.

104
00:07:46,224 --> 00:07:47,450
Key takeaways.

105
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:53,668
Statistics hide structure, so always plot; remember Anscombe's quartet.

106
00:07:53,668 --> 00:07:58,923
Every chart is data, aesthetics, geometry, scales and theme.

107
00:07:58,923 --> 00:08:04,528
Choose the geometry from the question and use facets for groups.

108
00:08:04,528 --> 00:08:10,133
Start bar axes at zero and avoid decoration that bends the data.

109
00:08:10,133 --> 00:08:16,175
Categories, hierarchies, text and networks each have a natural chart.

110
00:08:16,175 --> 00:08:21,605
And dashboards have one purpose, while stories have a message.

111
00:08:21,605 --> 00:08:25,896
In the lab you will build all of these in Python.

112
00:08:25,896 --> 00:08:27,648
Thanks for watching.

